// The browser: a native 3-D browser in Cone, in the spirit of Pegasus3D.
// The shell and the walkable plane: an endless brown ground under a
// sky-blue sky, in metres, walked on at eye height; and a world in it, baked
// in for now (bakedworld/), standing in for a fetched one: it reaches the
// browser only through a Door, reserving ids and sending the same requests
// the shell uses for its own ground and sky.
//
// IT IS FOUR ACTORS. Each owns its state, and they reach each other only by
// messages (Cone's actors, the actors package):
//
//   shell    this file, on the main thread: the window, the input and the
//            user's controller, the clock and the fixed steps, the frame's
//            statistics, and the scripted run's and the watched run's
//            events. It is not a Cone actor, because SDL wants the window's
//            events on the thread that made the window and an actor runs
//            on whichever worker takes it up; its mailboxes are two
//            channels the scene answers into.
//   scene    sceneactor.cone: the scene's registry, tables and clock, the
//            beings, their bodies and possession (the physics, for now),
//            the birds, picking; it publishes the snapshot
//   world    worldactor.cone: the world's own code (the baked-in world),
//            given a Door of its own ("world" is a placeholder name)
//   render   renderactor.cone: the device and everything on it; it draws
//            the snapshot and presents
//
// A FRAME, as the messages go:
//
//   shell    input: each event into the input package; the actions into
//            the user's intents; the steps due on the clock
//     -> scene.frame(input)
//   scene    simulate: the steps (the intents to the possessed being, every
//            being, the birds, the scene's clock); publish: the keys, a
//            click picked, timers and areas into the world's mail, then
//     -> world.tick(the registry, the mail, the time), awaited
//   world    its next object hydrated and injected, its mail read, its
//            motions sent: the registry and its requests handed back
//   scene    the beings placed; the apply point: its own requests applied,
//            render's checked and kept in order; the world matrices; the
//            SNAPSHOT published, then
//     -> render.draw(the snapshot, render's requests), awaited
//   render   render's requests applied, the frame drawn from the snapshot
//            alone, presented, the next image acquired: the snapshot
//            handed back (and, scripted, what it drew)
//   scene    a scripted run's checks and its next events
//     -> the shell's channel: the frame's report (the phases, as each
//            actor timed its own; the sizes a watched run prints)
//
// The scene awaits the world and render in turn, so one actor runs at a
// time and a frame is the same sequence it was when this was one loop: the
// picture, the checks and the timings are those of the single thread. No
// actor divides its own work across threads, and none runs ahead of the
// others; those wait for Cone's parallel joins.
//
// THE SCENE'S TIME is the scene's clock (parts/events.cone): the
// simulation's fixed steps at the clock's rate (1; 0 paused with P; 10
// with F), exact on the synthetic clock. The world's motions, the effects,
// the timers and the day clock are read from it; the walk is not.
//
// Arguments, besides frame's (--frames N, --headless, --quiet):
//   --script        walk, turn and click with no one at the keyboard: events
//                   pushed into SDL's own queue (sdl.PushEvent), never the
//                   system's, and checked against what they must do (script.cone)
//   --shots DIR     write the scripted run's views into DIR as BMPs
//   --watch S, --soak N   a watched real-time run, a soak (watch.cone)
//
// A run of a fixed number of frames (--frames, --script) takes input only
// from its own pushed events, so it is the same every time.

